From ce5784d31cd5b67188f55ad4296c2b3de047dee6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ciro Iriarte <ciro.iriarte@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 4 Jul 2026 21:37:04 -0300
Subject: [PATCH 08/11] terminal: add flow control to text-output via ack
 backpressure

Track the number of text-output blobs sent to the connection owner but
not yet acknowledged. When that reaches a bound
(GUAC_TERMINAL_TEXT_OUTPUT_MAX_INFLIGHT), further buffered output is
dropped rather than sent, so a stalled or slow text-output consumer
cannot cause unbounded buffering.

Output is dropped rather than blocked deliberately: in tee mode the raw
stream shares the protocol read loop with the graphical display, so
blocking the source to backpressure the text consumer would also stall
any co-attached browser user. True blocking backpressure (safe only when
the CLI is the sole consumer) belongs to the headless raw mode (#23).

The stream now carries an ack handler that decrements the outstanding
count as blobs are acknowledged. The manual e2e harness is extended to
acknowledge blobs (with a --no-ack switch and --expect-absent assertion)
and to exercise both directions: a flood delivered in full when acked,
and dropped backlog when unacked.

Refs #22

Signed-off-by: Ciro Iriarte <ciro.iriarte@gmail.com>
