From d746ddc5a19a4e861b36faf2ad1ba4e4df90e1d6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ciro Iriarte <ciro.iriarte@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 4 Jul 2026 23:38:57 -0300
Subject: [PATCH 09/11] terminal: add headless "text-output=raw" mode (suppress
 rendering)

Extend the text-output parameter to accept "raw" in addition to "true":

- "true" keeps the existing tee behaviour (raw bytes to the STDOUT pipe
  AND the graphical display rendered for browser clients).
- "raw" is headless: the graphical terminal is not rendered from remote
  output. Each protocol read loop still tees the raw bytes to the pipe
  but skips guac_terminal_write(), so no glyph parsing, rasterization,
  image encoding, or graphical instructions are produced.

For terminal-heavy interactive output this removes the dominant cost.
Measured on a color-diverse workload, raw mode holds graphical output to
a small constant init residual (~1 KB) regardless of volume, versus
~100 KB+ for the same workload in tee mode, while delivering all text.

Because raw mode does not render, the graphical frame cycle that normally
flushes the text-output buffer never runs; a text_output_flush_immediately
flag makes writes flush as they arrive so small outputs are not withheld
until the buffer fills or the stream closes.

Parsing accepts "true"/"raw" (any other value disables text-output),
keeping existing "true" connections fully backward compatible. The
disable-copy gate still applies. The manual e2e harness gains graphical-
byte accounting (--max-graphics) and raw-mode checks for all three
protocols proving text is delivered while graphics is suppressed.

Note: raw mode still allocates the terminal (for input/size/stdin
plumbing); eliminating that one-time allocation is a separate follow-up.

Refs #23

Signed-off-by: Ciro Iriarte <ciro.iriarte@gmail.com>
